In Java, the == operator compares the memory addresses of the two objects, while the .equals() method compares the contents of the two objects. This means that == will always return false if the two objects refer to different locations in memory, while .equals() will only return false if the two objects have different values.

For example, consider the following code:

String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";

if (s1 == s2) {
    // both strings refer to the same location in memory
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 are equal");
} else {
    // s1 and s2 refer to different locations in memory
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 are not equal");
}

// s1 and s2 are not equal

On the other hand, consider the following code:

String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";

if (s1.equals(s2)) {
    // both strings have the same value
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 are equal");
} else {
    // s1 and s2 have different values
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 are not equal");
}

// s1 and s2 are equal

As you can see, == will always return false if the two objects refer to different locations in memory, while .equals() will only return false if the two objects have different values. Therefore, it is generally a good idea to use .equals() instead of == when comparing strings in Java.

