The Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) problem is a classical problem in string matching. Here is an implementation in Java:
```java
public static int longestCommonSubsequence(String s1, String s2) {
    int[][] dp = new int[s1.length()][s2.length()];
    int n = s1.length();
    int m = s2.length();

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            if (s1.charAt(i) == s2.charAt(j)) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
    }

    return dp[n - 1][m - 1];
}
```
The above implementation uses dynamic programming to solve the problem. The idea is to create an array dp[][] of size n x m, where n is the length of the first string and m is the length of the second string. The value dp[i][j] represents the length of the longest common subsequence that starts at index i in the first string and ends at index j in the second string.

We initialize dp[][] with all zeros. Then we loop through the first string, and for each character we check if it is present in the second string. If it is present, we update dp[][] with the current value plus 1. If the character is not present in the second string, we update dp[][] with the maximum of the current value and the value of the previous row plus 1.

Finally, we return the value of dp[n-1][m-1], which represents the length of the longest common subsequence.

